libcamera: software_isp: highlight-priority auto exposure

The OnePlus 7 Pro main camera exposes a dim room with a big sunlit window for
the room: the mean sample value of the histogram is driven to the sensor's
target, and the window, 13% of the frame, renders white. The highlight
headroom of 0018 keeps the raw data of those pixels from clipping, but it
gives the stops back as digital gain with a shoulder that maps the sensor's
full range to white, so the output still clips. A reference phone exposes for
the window instead: sky, water and buildings keep their colour and the room
goes dark.

Agc now reads two optional keys under "- Agc:". maxClipped is the share of the
output allowed to render near white, 0.001 to 0.5, and its absence keeps the
old behaviour exactly. maxHighlightEv is how many stops the feature may take
off the target, 0 to 4, 2.5 by default. Out of range values are ignored with a
warning.

On every update the algorithm estimates the share of pixels that render near
white. The statistics' histogram is of raw luma, before the white balance: a
neutral highlight's channels are scaled apart there, so its luma is the level
the gains bring it to times 0.299/gainR + 0.587 + 0.114/gainB, 0.79 with the
red gain 2.4 and blue 1.5 a tungsten-lit room takes, and a window whose green
is at full scale never reaches the top of the histogram. The estimate divides
that factor back out from the current gains, clamps the result where the
debayer clamps after its gains, and follows its chain to the display: the
headroom's digital gain with the same shoulder, the contrast S-curve and the
gamma, both from the knobs Adjust::prepare() reads (the two exponents are now
shared through adjust.h). A pixel counts from 250 of 255. A new highlightEv
state, kept in IPAActiveState::agc so a configure resets it, steps down by
0.25 stop while that share is over maxClipped and back up by 0.1 stop once it
falls under half of it, clamped to [-maxHighlightEv, 0]. highlightEv is added
to the ExposureValue the target is scaled by, so the loop converges darker and
stops inside that hysteresis band. Manual exposure and gain still bypass it,
as they bypass the headroom, and the headroom keeps working at the new
exposure, where it needs fewer stops. Nothing is published to applications: a
debug line reports the near-white share, the neutral factor, the first
histogram bin that counts and the stops in force.
